WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.030
 

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:10.290
RL: Here we see cells flowing through the bloodstream.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.070
But let's focus on this rolling macrophage

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.880
that sticks to the endothelial cells that line the blood

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.610
vessels by adhering to proteins.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.540
It's almost like molecular velcro.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.510
The surface of the cell is remarkably dynamic,

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.200
with membrane proteins that form connections with other cells,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.670
and respond to external signals.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.270
If we flip across the membrane and look on the inside of the cell,

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:37.750
we'll see this remarkable internal architecture, made up

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.220
of the cytoskeleton.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.610
It's very similar to the structural elements that's

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:46.630
holding up the ceiling over your head, but unlike those structural elements,

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.650
these are very dynamic.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:51.900
They assemble, and in this case, we're looking

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:55.940
at an acting filament, or several acting filaments assembling,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.080
but they can also disassemble when other proteins sever

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:01.280
those structural elements.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:04.090
 

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.590
Here we're looking at a microtubule, which is much more complex,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:13.680
but it too assembles and disassembles in some truly remarkable ways.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:16.840
These structural elements also serve as highways

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:21.640
that motor proteins use to drag, for example, vesicles.

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.800
These vesicles are full of proteins and other macromolecules that

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.560
are distributed throughout the cytoplasm of the cell.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.120
 

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.820
Messenger RNAs are transported out of the nucleus,

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:38.480
and serve as templates for protein translation.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.310
So ribosomes assemble on these messenger RNAs,

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:45.100
and as those proteins are made, some of them

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:50.470
are free to float in the cytoplasm, while others bind to other proteins

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:54.550
that help target them to organelles, such as this mitochondrion

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.890
that you're seeing right now.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.960
Still other proteins are translated directly

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.300
into other organelles like the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:08.139
The endoplasmic reticulum releases vesicles once again,

00:02:08.139 --> 00:02:11.790
containing proteins and other macromolecules.

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.120
So here we see the Golgi apparatus that's

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:18.320
receiving vesicles and also releasing vesicles, some of which

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.460
fuse with the plasma membrane and dump their cargo

00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:23.470
into the external environments.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:29.000
And also release transmembrane proteins into the plasma membrane.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:32.870
These proteins can change their shape dramatically in response

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.920
to external signals.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.290
These changes in shape allow them to bind

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:41.030
to other proteins, which in the case of this macrophage,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:46.580
causes it to stop rolling, dramatically change shape, and squeeze in

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.710
between two neighboring endothelial cells

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.070
to go and fight infection in the tissue.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:03:18.571
 

